package com.sovereignedge.pocketcoach.data

/** How-to text for the built-in library, keyed by exercise name. Seeded into `exercises.description`. */
object ExerciseGuide {
    val DESCRIPTIONS: Map<String, String> = mapOf(
        "Back Squat" to "Bar on the upper back, feet about shoulder width, toes slightly out. Brace, sit down and back until the hip crease is below the knee, keep the chest up and knees tracking over the toes, then drive the floor away. Keep the whole foot planted.",
        "Front Squat" to "Bar rests on the front of the shoulders with elbows high and fingertips under the bar. Squat straight down keeping the torso upright and elbows up; if the elbows drop the bar rolls forward. Stand up driving the elbows towards the ceiling.",
        "Box Squat" to "Set a box at or just below parallel. Sit back onto the box under control, pause for a beat without relaxing, then stand up explosively. Shins stay close to vertical; don't rock off the box.",
        "Pause Squat" to "A back squat with a deliberate 2–3 second pause in the bottom. Stay tight and keep the chest up during the pause, then drive up without bouncing. Use less weight than a normal squat.",
        "Overhead Squat" to "Bar locked out overhead with a wide snatch grip, armpits forward. Squat down keeping the bar over the middle of the foot; the torso stays as upright as mobility allows. Requires good shoulder and ankle mobility, so start light.",
        "Goblet Squat" to "Hold a dumbbell or kettlebell against the chest with both hands. Squat deep with elbows inside the knees, chest tall, then stand. A good warm-up and teaching squat.",
        "Bulgarian Split Squat" to "Rear foot on a bench, front foot far enough forward that the front shin stays near vertical. Lower until the rear knee nearly touches the floor, then push through the front heel. Keep the torso upright or slightly forward; do all reps on one side then switch.",
        "Walking Lunge" to "Step forward and lower until both knees are at about 90 degrees, the rear knee just above the floor. Push through the front foot and step straight into the next lunge. Keep the torso tall and the front knee over the foot.",
        "Reverse Lunge" to "Step backwards and lower the rear knee towards the floor, keeping the front shin vertical. Push through the front heel to return. Easier on the knees than forward lunges.",
        "Step-Up" to "Place the whole foot on a box around knee height. Drive through that foot to stand up tall without pushing off the trailing leg, then lower under control. Keep the knee tracking over the toes.",
        "Leg Press" to "Feet shoulder width on the platform, lower back and hips stay on the pad. Lower the sled until the knees are at about 90 degrees or until the hips start to lift, then press without locking the knees out hard.",
        "Leg Extension" to "Sit with the pad on the lower shins and the knee joint lined up with the machine's pivot. Extend the legs fully, squeeze the quads at the top, then lower under control.",
        "Deadlift" to "Bar over the middle of the foot, shins touching the bar, hips higher than the knees, shoulders over the bar. Brace, pull the slack out, then push the floor away keeping the bar against the legs and the back flat. Stand tall; lower by hinging at the hips.",
        "Sumo Deadlift" to "Wide stance with toes out, hands inside the knees. Push the knees out, keep the torso more upright than a conventional pull, and drive the floor apart as the bar leaves the floor. Keep the bar close.",
        "Trap Bar Deadlift" to "Stand in the middle of the bar, grip the handles, hips down and chest up. Push through the whole foot and stand tall. More knee bend and a more upright torso than a straight bar deadlift.",
        "Romanian Deadlift" to "Start standing with the bar. Push the hips back with a slight knee bend, sliding the bar down the thighs until you feel a stretch in the hamstrings, usually just below the knee. Keep the back flat and the bar close, then drive the hips forward to stand.",
        "Single-Leg Romanian Deadlift" to "Stand on one leg holding a dumbbell. Hinge at the hip, letting the free leg travel back as the torso comes forward, keeping the hips square. Return by driving the hip forward. Light loads; balance first.",
        "Good Morning" to "Bar on the upper back, soft knees. Hinge at the hips pushing them back until the torso is around 45 degrees or you feel the hamstrings, keeping the back flat, then return. Keep it light and controlled.",
        "Hip Thrust" to "Upper back on a bench, bar across the hips (use a pad), feet flat so the shins are vertical at the top. Drive the hips up until the body is a straight line from shoulders to knees, chin tucked, squeeze the glutes, then lower.",
        "Glute Bridge" to "Lie on your back, knees bent, feet flat. Drive through the heels to lift the hips until the body is straight from shoulders to knees, squeeze the glutes at the top, lower slowly.",
        "Kettlebell Swing" to "Hinge, hike the bell back between the legs, then snap the hips forward to float the bell to chest height. The arms are ropes; the power comes from the hips. Let it fall and hinge again; don't squat it.",
        "Nordic Hamstring Curl" to "Kneel with the ankles anchored. Keeping the hips extended and body straight, lower the torso towards the floor as slowly as possible using the hamstrings, catch yourself with the hands, then push back lightly to return.",
        "Leg Curl" to "Pad just above the heels. Curl the heels towards the glutes, squeezing the hamstrings, then lower under control without letting the weight stack touch.",
        "Back Extension" to "Hips on the pad with the pad just below the hip bones. Lower the torso by hinging at the hips, then raise until the body is straight; don't hyperextend. Add a plate across the chest for load.",
        "Bench Press" to "Eyes under the bar, shoulder blades pulled back and down, feet flat. Unrack, lower the bar to the lower chest with elbows at about 45–75 degrees, touch without bouncing, then press back up and slightly towards the face. Keep the glutes on the bench.",
        "Close-Grip Bench Press" to "A bench press with hands about shoulder width apart. Keep the elbows tucked and lower the bar to the lower chest. Emphasises the triceps; use less weight than a normal bench.",
        "Incline Bench Press" to "Bench set to 30–45 degrees. Lower the bar to the upper chest with elbows tucked slightly, then press up. Keep the shoulder blades pulled back throughout.",
        "Dumbbell Bench Press" to "Lie back with dumbbells over the chest, palms forward. Lower them to the sides of the chest with elbows at about 45 degrees, then press up and slightly together. Keep the shoulder blades pinned.",
        "Incline Dumbbell Press" to "On a 30–45 degree bench, lower the dumbbells to the upper chest and press up. Keep the wrists stacked over the elbows and don't flare the elbows straight out.",
        "Overhead Press" to "Bar on the front of the shoulders, grip just outside the shoulders, glutes and abs tight. Press straight up, moving the head back slightly to let the bar pass, then push the head through at lockout so the bar finishes over the middle of the foot.",
        "Push Press" to "Same setup as the overhead press. Dip a few inches by bending the knees with an upright torso, then drive up with the legs and press the bar overhead in one motion. Lower under control.",
        "Dumbbell Shoulder Press" to "Seated or standing, dumbbells at shoulder height with palms forward. Press overhead until the arms lock out, then lower to the shoulders. Keep the ribs down and don't lean back.",
        "Push-Up" to "Hands just wider than the shoulders, body in a straight line from head to heels. Lower the chest to the floor with elbows at about 45 degrees, then press back up. Squeeze the glutes to keep the hips from sagging.",
        "Dip" to "Support yourself on parallel bars. Lower until the upper arms are about parallel to the floor, leaning slightly forward for more chest or staying upright for triceps, then press up. Add weight with a belt.",
        "Floor Press" to "Lie on the floor with a bar held over the chest. Lower until the upper arms touch the floor, pause, then press. Takes the legs out of the lift and limits the range of motion, good for the lockout.",
        "Lateral Raise" to "Dumbbells at the sides, slight bend in the elbows. Raise the arms out to the sides until they are level with the shoulders, leading with the elbows, then lower slowly. Keep the traps relaxed; light weight.",
        "Triceps Pushdown" to "Cable at the top, elbows pinned to the sides. Push the handle down until the arms are straight, squeeze, then let it return without the elbows drifting forward.",
        "Skull Crusher" to "Lying on a bench with an EZ bar or dumbbells over the chest. Bend only at the elbows to lower the weight towards the forehead or just behind it, then extend. Keep the upper arms still.",
        "Pull-Up" to "Hang from a bar with an overhand grip a little wider than the shoulders. Pull the chest towards the bar by driving the elbows down, chin over the bar, then lower all the way to straight arms. Avoid swinging.",
        "Chin-Up" to "Like a pull-up with an underhand, shoulder-width grip. Pull until the chin clears the bar, lower fully. Slightly more biceps involvement than a pull-up.",
        "Lat Pulldown" to "Grip the bar wider than the shoulders, thighs under the pads. Pull the bar to the upper chest while leaning back slightly, driving the elbows down, then let the arms extend fully. Don't swing the torso.",
        "Barbell Row" to "Hinge forward to around 45 degrees or lower with a flat back, bar hanging below the shoulders. Pull the bar to the lower chest or upper stomach, squeeze the shoulder blades, then lower under control. Keep the torso still.",
        "Pendlay Row" to "Torso parallel to the floor, bar on the floor each rep. Pull explosively to the lower chest, then lower back to a dead stop on the floor. Strict; no torso movement.",
        "Dumbbell Row" to "One hand and knee on a bench, back flat. Row the dumbbell to the hip, driving the elbow back and up, squeeze, then lower fully. Keep the shoulders square to the floor.",
        "Seated Cable Row" to "Sit tall with knees slightly bent. Pull the handle to the stomach, keeping the torso upright and squeezing the shoulder blades together, then let the arms extend without rounding forward.",
        "Chest-Supported Row" to "Chest on an incline bench or machine pad so the lower back is taken out of it. Row the weight towards the chest, pause with the shoulder blades squeezed, then lower.",
        "Inverted Row" to "Hang under a bar or rings with the body straight and heels on the floor. Pull the chest to the bar keeping the body rigid, then lower. Walk the feet forward to make it easier.",
        "Face Pull" to "Rope at face height. Pull the rope towards the face with the elbows high and wide, finishing with the hands beside the ears and the shoulder blades squeezed. Light weight, controlled.",
        "Biceps Curl" to "Dumbbells or bar at the sides, elbows pinned. Curl up while keeping the upper arms still, squeeze at the top, then lower all the way. Don't swing the torso.",
        "Hammer Curl" to "Dumbbells held with palms facing each other. Curl up keeping the neutral grip and elbows still, then lower under control. Works the brachialis and forearms.",
        "Barbell Curl" to "Shoulder-width underhand grip on a bar. Curl up with elbows pinned at the sides, squeeze, then lower fully. Keep the torso upright.",
        "Clean" to "From the floor, pull the bar past the knees keeping it close, then explosively extend the hips and shrug, pulling yourself under into a full front squat with the bar on the shoulders. Stand up. Technical; learn it with a coach and light weights.",
        "Power Clean" to "A clean caught high, above a quarter squat. Pull from the floor, extend violently, and catch the bar on the shoulders with a small dip, then stand. Reset each rep.",
        "Hang Clean" to "Start standing with the bar at the thighs. Hinge slightly, then extend the hips hard, shrug and catch the bar on the shoulders. Removes the first pull so you can focus on the explosion.",
        "Clean and Jerk" to "Clean the bar to the shoulders, stand, reset. Then dip and drive the bar overhead, splitting or pushing the feet out to catch it locked out, and recover to standing. Learn it with a coach.",
        "Snatch" to "Wide grip. Pull from the floor, extend the hips explosively and pull under the bar in one motion, catching it locked out overhead in a full squat, then stand. The most technical lift; start with a PVC pipe.",
        "Power Snatch" to "A snatch caught above parallel. Pull, extend, and catch overhead with a small dip. Reset each rep.",
        "Hang Snatch" to "Start standing with the bar at the thighs and a wide grip. Hinge slightly, extend hard and catch overhead. Removes the first pull.",
        "Split Jerk" to "Bar on the shoulders. Dip straight down a few inches, drive hard through the legs and punch the bar overhead while splitting the feet front and back. Lock out, then bring the front foot back and the rear foot forward.",
        "Clean Pull" to "The first and second pull of a clean without catching it: pull from the floor, extend the hips fully and shrug, then lower. Heavier than your clean; builds the pull.",
        "Snatch Pull" to "The pull of a snatch with a wide grip and no catch: pull from the floor, extend fully and shrug, then lower under control.",
        "Plank" to "Forearms on the floor, elbows under the shoulders, body straight from head to heels. Squeeze the glutes and brace the abs so the hips don't sag or pike. Breathe.",
        "Side Plank" to "On one forearm with the elbow under the shoulder, feet stacked, hips lifted so the body is a straight line. Hold, then switch sides.",
        "Dead Bug" to "Lie on your back with arms straight up and knees over the hips. Press the lower back into the floor, then slowly extend one arm overhead and the opposite leg out, return, and alternate. Keep the back flat the whole time.",
        "Hanging Leg Raise" to "Hang from a bar. Raise the legs (bent or straight) until the thighs are at least parallel, curling the pelvis up at the top, then lower slowly without swinging.",
        "Ab Wheel Rollout" to "Kneel with the wheel under the shoulders. Roll forward slowly keeping the hips extended and the abs braced, as far as you can without the lower back arching, then pull back.",
        "Pallof Press" to "Stand side-on to a cable at chest height, handle held at the chest with both hands. Press the hands straight out in front and hold while resisting the pull that wants to turn you, then bring them back. Switch sides.",
        "Russian Twist" to "Sit with knees bent and feet lifted or on the floor, leaning back slightly. Holding a weight, rotate the torso to tap it beside each hip, keeping the chest up.",
        "Cable Crunch" to "Kneel facing a cable with a rope held beside the head. Crunch down by rounding the upper back, bringing the elbows towards the knees, then return under control. Keep the hips still.",
        "Farmer's Carry" to "Pick up a heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in each hand. Walk tall with the shoulders back, ribs down and quick steps for the distance or time. Set them down with a flat back.",
        "Suitcase Carry" to "Carry one heavy weight at your side and walk without leaning towards or away from it. Works the obliques; switch hands halfway.",
        "Sled Push" to "Hands on the sled posts, arms straight or bent, lean in and drive with the legs taking powerful steps. Keep the back flat and the hips low.",
        "Sled Drag" to "Strap or rope attached to the sled, walk forwards or backwards taking strong steps. Backwards drags are great for the quads and knees.",
        "Box Jump" to "Stand a short step from a box. Swing the arms, dip, and jump, landing softly on the box with the whole foot and knees over the toes. Step down, don't jump down.",
        "Broad Jump" to "Swing the arms back, dip, and jump forward as far as possible, landing softly with bent knees. Reset for each rep.",
        "Depth Jump" to "Step off a low box, land on both feet and immediately jump as high as possible with minimal ground contact time. Advanced; keep the box low.",
        "Medicine Ball Slam" to "Lift the ball overhead on tiptoes, then slam it into the floor as hard as possible using the whole body. Catch it on the bounce or pick it up and repeat.",
        "Rowing" to "On the erg: push with the legs first, then swing the torso back, then pull the handle to the lower ribs. Return in reverse order: arms, body, legs. Drive hard, recover slowly.",
        "Assault Bike" to "Sit tall and drive with both the legs and the arms. Pace yourself for intervals; it is harder than it looks.",
        "Run" to "Easy runs should be at a conversational pace. For intervals, warm up first, run the work bouts at the target pace and jog or walk the recoveries.",
        "Sprint" to "After a thorough warm-up, run the distance at or near maximal effort with a tall posture and relaxed face and hands. Walk back for full recovery between reps.",
        "Jump Rope" to "Elbows close to the sides, turn the rope from the wrists and bounce lightly on the balls of the feet. Keep the jumps small.",
        "Burpee" to "From standing, drop the hands to the floor, jump the feet back to a push-up position, lower the chest to the floor, push up, jump the feet back in and jump up with the arms overhead.",
        "Wall Ball" to "Hold a medicine ball at the chest facing a wall. Squat to full depth, then stand and throw the ball to the target height in one motion, catching it and dropping straight into the next squat.",
        "Battle Rope" to "Hold a rope end in each hand with a slight squat. Make waves by alternating or double-arm slams for the time, keeping the core braced.",
        "Calf Raise" to "Balls of the feet on a step, heels hanging. Rise onto the toes as high as possible, pause, then lower until you feel a stretch in the calves. Straight knees for the gastrocnemius; bent for the soleus.",
        "Hip Abduction" to "On the machine, push the knees apart against the pads, pause, and return under control. Works the glute medius.",
        "Shrug" to "Hold a bar or dumbbells at the sides. Shrug the shoulders straight up towards the ears, pause, then lower. Don't roll the shoulders.",
        "Band Pull-Apart" to "Hold a light band in front at shoulder height with straight arms. Pull it apart until it touches the chest, squeezing the shoulder blades, then return slowly.",
        "Couch Stretch" to "Kneel with one shin up against a wall or couch, the other foot forward. Squeeze the glute of the rear leg and bring the torso upright to stretch the hip flexor and quad. Hold and breathe.",
        "Hip 90/90" to "Sit with the front leg bent to 90 degrees in front and the rear leg bent to 90 degrees to the side. Keep the chest tall and lean forward over the front shin, then switch sides.",
        "Thoracic Rotation" to "On hands and knees, place one hand behind the head and rotate the elbow up towards the ceiling, following it with the eyes, then bring it down towards the opposite hand. Move from the upper back.",
        "Cable Fly" to "Stand between two cables set at chest height or above with a slight forward lean. With a soft bend in the elbows, bring the hands together in front of the chest in an arc, squeeze, then open back out under control.",
        "Overhead Triceps Extension" to "Hold a dumbbell or rope overhead with the elbows close to the head. Lower the weight behind the head by bending only at the elbows, then extend back up. Keep the upper arms still.",
        "Cycling" to "Easy rides should be at a conversational pace with a cadence around 85–95 rpm. Keep the upper body relaxed, fuel on rides over an hour.",
    )
}
